lid #b08d2e;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 18px"><strong>Short answer:</strong> To compare two PDFs for free, open TheEditPDF's Compare PDFs tool and choose the first PDF (Document A) and the second PDF (Document B). Both render side by side starting at page 1 — use Prev and Next to step through pages together and spot differences visually. Both files are rendered locally in your browser — nothing is uploaded.</p><h2>Compare two PDFs side by side</h2><p>Reviewing contract revisions, checking a new draft against the old one, or verifying that a conversion didn't change anything? TheEditPDF's free Compare PDFs tool loads two documents and shows the same page of each side by side, with simple page navigation. Both files are rendered locally in your browser — nothing is uploaded, so even confidential drafts stay private.</p><h3>How to compare PDFs</h3><ol><li>Choose the first PDF (Document A) and the second PDF (Document B).</li><li>Both render side by side starting at page 1.</li><li>Use Prev / Next to step through pages together and spot differences visually.</li></ol><h3>When visual comparison wins</h3><p>Automated diff tools choke on scanned pages, complex layouts, and design-heavy documents. Your eyes don't: side-by-side viewing instantly reveals moved paragraphs, changed figures, altered totals, and formatting shifts. It's the fastest way to answer "what changed between v1 and v2?" for anything from legal redlines to invoice revisions.</p><p class="faq-q">Do the documents need the same page count?</p><p>No. Navigation runs to the shorter document's length; extra pages in the longer file are simply beyond the comparison range.</p><p class="faq-q">Is there an automatic highlighted diff?</p><p>This tool shows pages side by side without pixel-diff highlighting, which keeps it fast and reliable for any document type, including scans.</p><p class="faq-q">Can I compare more than two files?</p><p>Two at a time. For three versions, compare them in pairs.</