 #b08d2e;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 18px"><strong>Short answer:</strong> To convert Excel to PDF for free, open TheEditPDF's Excel to PDF tool, choose your Excel file (.xlsx or .xls), and click Convert to PDF. Your workbook is frozen into a clean, shareable document that looks the same everywhere — right in your browser, with no upload and no account.</p><h2>Convert Excel spreadsheets to PDF</h2><p>Spreadsheets are easy to break — one wrong sort and the data's a mess. Converting to PDF freezes your workbook into a clean, shareable document that looks the same everywhere. TheEditPDF's free Excel to PDF tool converts .xlsx and .xls files right in your browser, with no upload and no account.</p><h3>How to convert Excel to PDF</h3><ol><li>Choose your Excel file (.xlsx or .xls).</li><li>Click "Convert to PDF".</li><li>Download the PDF version of your spreadsheet.</li></ol><h3>What the PDF looks like</h3><p>Each worksheet becomes PDF pages with your cell values, basic formatting, and column layout preserved. It's ideal for sending invoices, reports, and price lists to people who shouldn't — or can't — edit the numbers. Because the file never leaves your device, sensitive financial data stays private.</p><p class="faq-q">Are formulas calculated in the PDF?</p><p>Yes — the converter reads computed cell values, so what you see in Excel is what appears in the PDF.</p><p class="faq-q">What about charts and images in the sheet?</p><p>Basic cell content converts reliably; complex charts and embedded objects may not carry over. For chart-heavy sheets, export charts as images separately.</p><p class="faq-q">Can I convert multiple sheets?</p><p>All worksheets in the workbook are included in order.</p>
