olid #b08d2e;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 18px"><strong>Short answer:</strong> To convert JPG images to PDF for free, open TheEditPDF's JPG to PDF tool, choose your JPG or PNG images, reorder them if needed, and click Create PDF. Each image fits neatly onto its own page in a single combined PDF, all processed locally in your browser.</p><h2>Combine JPG and PNG images into one PDF</h2><p>Turn photos of documents, scanned receipts, or design mockups into a single tidy PDF. TheEditPDF's free JPG to PDF tool accepts JPG and PNG images, fits each one neatly onto its own page, and downloads a combined PDF — all processed locally in your browser.</p><h3>How to convert images to PDF</h3><ol><li>Choose your JPG or PNG images (select several at once).</li><li>Reorder them with the arrow buttons if needed.</li><li>Click "Create PDF" and download the result.</li></ol><h3>Smart details</h3><p>Each image gets its own page sized to the image, scaled down only if it exceeds a sensible maximum — so quality is preserved. This is perfect for expense reports (snap receipts, get one PDF), portfolios, and archiving whiteboard photos. PNG transparency is flattened against white, which is what PDF readers expect.</p><p class="faq-q">Can I mix JPG and PNG in one PDF?</p><p>Yes — select both kinds together and they will be combined in the order you arrange them.</p><p class="faq-q">Will my photos be compressed?</p><p>Images are embedded at high quality. Very large photos are scaled to a maximum dimension to keep the PDF manageable.</p><p class="faq-q">What if my images have different sizes?</p><p>Each image keeps its own aspect ratio on its own page, so mixed sizes are handled gracefully — nothing is stretched.</p><p class="faq-q">Can I add a password to the resulting PDF?</p><p>Browser-based encryption isn't supported by the underlying library, so protect the file with your operating system's tools if needed.</p>
