 #b08d2e;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 18px"><strong>Short answer:</strong> To merge PDF files into one PDF for free, open TheEditPDF's Merge PDF tool, select two or more PDFs, arrange them in the order you want, and download the combined file. There is no fixed limit on the number of files, and pages are copied exactly with their original fonts, images, and layout. Your files never leave your browser, and no signup is needed.</p><h2>Combine multiple PDFs into one file</h2><p>Merging PDFs is one of the most common document chores: job applications with a cover letter plus CV, expense reports with scattered receipts, or chapters of a report that need to become a single file. TheEditPDF's free Merge PDF tool joins any number of PDFs in the exact order you choose, entirely in your browser. Nothing is uploaded, so even sensitive financial or legal files stay private.</p><h3>How to merge PDFs</h3><ol><li>Select two or more PDF files (or drag them into the upload area).</li><li>Use the arrow buttons to reorder them — the top file comes first.</li><li>Click "Merge &amp; download".</li><li>Save the combined PDF to your device.</li></ol><h3>Why merge with TheEditPDF?</h3><p>Many online mergers cap the number of files, add watermarks, or queue your job on a busy server. TheEditPDF merges locally, so there is no waiting room and no quality loss: pages are copied exactly as they are, with their original fonts, images, and layout intact. It works offline once the page has loaded, and on any device with a modern browser.</p><p class="faq-q">Is there a limit on the number of files?</p><p>No fixed limit — the practical constraint is your device's memory. Merging dozens of typical documents works without trouble.</p><p class="faq-q">Will bookmarks or form fields survive merging?</p><p>Page content, fonts, and images are preserved exactly. Interactive elements like form fields may be flattened depending on the source files.</p>
