solid #b08d2e;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 18px"><strong>Short answer:</strong> To edit PDF metadata for free, open TheEditPDF's PDF Metadata tool and choose your PDF — its current title, author, subject, and keywords appear automatically. Edit any field, or clear fields you want to remove, then click Save metadata and download the updated file. Nothing is uploaded, so your documents stay private.</p><h2>View and edit PDF metadata</h2><p>Every PDF carries hidden metadata — title, author, subject, and keywords — that shows up in search results, file managers, and document properties. TheEditPDF's free PDF Metadata tool lets you inspect and rewrite these fields in seconds, right in your browser. Nothing is uploaded, so your documents stay private.</p><h3>How to edit PDF metadata</h3><ol><li>Choose your PDF file — its current title, author, subject, and keywords appear automatically.</li><li>Edit any field, or clear fields you want to remove.</li><li>Click "Save metadata" and download the updated file.</li></ol><h3>Why metadata matters</h3><p>Proper metadata makes documents findable: a report titled "Document1" is invisible in a crowded drive, while "Q3 Sales Report — Acme Corp" is instantly recognizable. Cleaning metadata also protects privacy — files often carry the author's name or the software used, which you may not want to share. Publishers and archivists use keywords to keep large collections searchable.</p><p class="faq-q">Does editing metadata change the document content?</p><p>No. Only the hidden properties change; every page stays byte-identical.</p><p class="faq-q">Can I remove the author name for privacy?</p><p>Yes — clear the Author field before saving, and the downloaded file won't carry it.</p><p class="faq-q">Will this affect how the PDF opens?</p><p>Not at all. Readers display the new title in the title bar and tabs, which is usually an improvement.</p>
