 solid #b08d2e;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 18px"><strong>Short answer:</strong> To repair a corrupted PDF for free, open TheEditPDF's Repair PDF tool, choose the damaged PDF file, and click Repair and download. The tool re-reads your file with error tolerance, salvaging every readable page and rebuilding a clean document. It works entirely in your browser, so even confidential files stay on your device.</p><h2>Fix corrupted and broken PDF files</h2><p>A PDF that won't open — after a bad download, a crashed save, or a failing USB stick — isn't necessarily lost. TheEditPDF's free Repair PDF tool re-reads your file with error tolerance, salvaging every readable page and rebuilding a clean document you can download. It works entirely in your browser, so even confidential files stay on your device.</p><h3>How to repair a PDF</h3><ol><li>Choose the damaged PDF file.</li><li>Click "Repair &amp; download".</li><li>Open the rebuilt file and check that your pages are back.</li></ol><h3>What repair can and can't do</h3><p>Repair excels at structural damage: truncated downloads, broken cross-reference tables, and mangled metadata are routinely fixed, often recovering the whole document. It cannot invent data that was never written — if half the file is zeros, those pages are gone for good. Always repair a copy and keep the original, in case a second attempt with different tools helps.</p><p class="faq-q">Will my text and images survive?</p><p>Everything readable is preserved exactly. Only the unreadable portions are dropped.</p><p class="faq-q">The file opens but shows an error. Should I still repair it?</p><p>Yes — repairing a flaky file now prevents it from becoming unreadable later, and the rebuilt file is usually smaller and faster to open.</p><p class="faq-q">Is there a size limit?</p><p>Only your device's memory. Large damaged files repair fine on a desktop browser.</p>
