olid #b08d2e;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 18px"><strong>Short answer:</strong> To convert a PDF to JPG for free, open TheEditPDF's PDF to JPG converter, choose your PDF, and click Convert and download ZIP. Every page is rendered as a sharp JPG image and delivered in one ZIP file. Conversion happens entirely in your browser, so your documents stay private.</p><h2>Turn PDF pages into JPG images</h2><p>Need a page from a PDF as a picture — for a presentation, a listing, or social media? TheEditPDF's free PDF to JPG converter renders every page as a sharp JPG image and delivers them in one ZIP file. Conversion happens entirely in your browser, so your documents stay private.</p><h3>How to convert PDF to JPG</h3><ol><li>Choose your PDF file.</li><li>Click "Convert &amp; download ZIP".</li><li>Unzip to get one JPG per page, in order.</li></ol><h3>Quality and uses</h3><p>Pages are rendered at high resolution, so text stays crisp and images stay clean — suitable for slides, thumbnails, and web use. JPGs are ideal when you need universal compatibility: every phone, editor, and website accepts them. If you need transparency or pixel-perfect graphics, convert and then re-save the images in your preferred editor.</p><p class="faq-q">Can I convert just one page?</p><p>Convert the whole file, then keep the page you need from the ZIP. Alternatively, use Split PDF first if your file is very large.</p><p class="faq-q">Will the images have a watermark?</p><p>No. TheEditPDF never adds watermarks to anything it produces.</p><p class="faq-q">What resolution are the images?</p><p>Pages render at 1.7x scale (about 122 DPI), which looks sharp on screens and in slides. For print-quality extraction, the original PDF remains your best source.</p><p class="faq-q">Do the JPGs keep the page order?</p><p>Yes — files are named page-001.jpg, page-002.jpg, and so on, so they sort correctly everywhere.</p>
