 solid #b08d2e;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 18px"><strong>Short answer:</strong> To convert a PDF to Word for free, open TheEditPDF's PDF to Word converter, choose your PDF, and click Convert to Word. Your document's text is extracted into a .docx file you can open in Microsoft Word, Google Docs, or LibreOffice — all processed locally in your browser, with no upload and no account.</p><h2>Convert PDF text into an editable Word document</h2><p>PDFs are great for sharing but awkward for editing. TheEditPDF's free PDF to Word converter extracts your document's text into a .docx file you can open in Microsoft Word, Google Docs, or LibreOffice — all processed locally in your browser, with no upload and no account.</p><h3>How to convert PDF to Word</h3><ol><li>Choose your PDF file.</li><li>Click "Convert to Word".</li><li>Download the .docx file and open it in your word processor.</li></ol><h3>What to expect</h3><p>The converter focuses on getting your words out accurately in reading order, with basic paragraph structure. Complex multi-column layouts, floating text boxes, and intricate tables may need tidying in Word afterward — that is true of every converter at any price. For simple reports, articles, contracts, and CVs, the result is usually ready to edit immediately.</p><p class="faq-q">Will images come across?</p><p>This converter is text-focused; images are not embedded in the Word file. For image extraction, use PDF to JPG.</p><p class="faq-q">What about scanned PDFs?</p><p>Scans contain no text layer to convert. Run the file through the OCR PDF tool first, then convert the resulting text.</p><p class="faq-q">Does it keep fonts and colors?</p><p>The Word file uses standard styling for maximum compatibility rather than trying to clone the PDF's exact typography.</p>
