eft:4px solid #b08d2e;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 18px"><strong>Short answer:</strong> To password-protect a PDF for free, open TheEditPDF's Protect PDF tool, choose the PDF, type a password of at least 4 characters and confirm it, and click Protect and download. The document is locked with AES-256 encryption, and anyone who opens the file will be asked for the password first. Everything happens in your browser, so your document is never uploaded to a server.</p><h2>Password-protect a PDF with AES-256 encryption</h2><p>Sending a contract, medical record, or financial statement by email? TheEditPDF's free Protect PDF tool locks your document with a password using AES-256 encryption — the same cipher standard trusted by banks and governments. Anyone who opens the file will be asked for the password first, in Adobe Reader, Chrome, Preview, or any PDF viewer. Everything happens in your browser, so your sensitive document is never uploaded to a server.</p><h3>How to protect a PDF</h3><ol><li>Choose the PDF you want to lock.</li><li>Type a password (at least 4 characters) and confirm it.</li><li>Optionally set a separate owner password and choose permissions for printing, copying, and modifying.</li><li>Click "Protect &amp; download" to save the encrypted file.</li></ol><h3>Security details</h3><p>The tool uses AES-256, the strongest encryption the PDF standard supports — far beyond the outdated 40-bit RC4 found in old protectors. The user password is required to open the file; the optional owner password grants full control regardless of the permission settings. Permissions let you allow printing while blocking text copying, or lock the document down entirely. Because encryption runs locally with the Web Crypto API, your password never leaves your device.</p><p class="faq-q">What if I forget the password?</p><p>There is no recovery — that is the point of real encryption. Store the password in a password manager before sharing the file.</p><p class="faq-q">Will the protected PDF open everywhere?</p><p>Yes. AES-256 password protection is part of the PDF standard, so the file opens in every modern reader once the password is entered.</p><p class="faq-q">Can I remove the password later?</p><p>Yes — open the file with its password and use the Unlock PDF tool to save an unprotected copy.</p><p class="faq-q">Is my file really private?</p><p>Completely. The PDF is encrypted on your own device; nothing is transmitted anywhere.</p>
