left:4px solid #b08d2e;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;margin:0 0 18px"><strong>Short answer:</strong> To unlock a PDF you are entitled to access, open TheEditPDF's Unlock PDF tool, choose the password-protected PDF, type the password when asked, and click Unlock and download. The tool rebuilds the PDF without the password and saves an unlocked copy — all in your browser, nothing uploaded.</p><h2>Remove a password from a PDF you own</h2><p>Locked out of your own document because the password is on a sticky note you lost? TheEditPDF's free Unlock PDF tool opens password-protected files you are entitled to access and saves an unlocked copy. Enter the password, and the tool rebuilds the PDF without it — all in your browser, nothing uploaded.</p><h3>How to unlock a PDF</h3><ol><li>Choose the password-protected PDF.</li><li>Type the password when asked.</li><li>Click "Unlock &amp; download" to save the unlocked file.</li></ol><h3>Important details</h3><p>You must know the password — this tool removes restrictions from files you can already open; it does not crack unknown passwords. Because of how browser PDF libraries handle encryption, pages are re-rendered as images in the unlocked copy: visually identical, but text is no longer selectable. Only use this on documents you own or are authorized to modify.</p><p class="faq-q">I forgot the password. Can this help?</p><p>No. Without the correct password the file can't be opened, and there is no legitimate browser tool that guesses passwords for you.</p><p class="faq-q">Why is the unlocked file bigger?</p><p>Pages become images during rebuilding, which increases size for text-heavy documents. The content looks exactly the same.</p><p class="faq-q">Is unlocking legal?</p><p>Removing a password from a document you own or are authorized to handle is fine. Don't use it on files you have no right to open.</p>
